\paragraph{Action distribution analysis.}
Figure~\ref{fig:death-action-dist} shows the action distribution in death versus non-death episodes for the internal strategy. Both episode types are dominated by \texttt{connect} actions ($87\%$ in death, $79\%$ in non-death), but \texttt{focus on} usage is $3{\times}$ higher in death episodes ($5.5\%$ vs.\ $1.8\%$). Among fatal \texttt{focus on} targets, \texttt{workshop} accounts for 44.6\%, suggesting a systematic bias toward specific objects rather than random exploration. Figure~\ref{fig:death-timeline} visualizes the temporal pattern: agents execute long sequences of \texttt{connect} actions before a terminal \texttt{focus on} triggers death, typically in the final 1--3 steps. Figure~\ref{fig:death-comparison} contrasts the internal and deterministic strategies: the deterministic strategy exhibits a qualitatively different action profile with near-zero \texttt{focus on} usage, confirming that the grounding failure is specific to LLM-generated action selection.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/death_action_distribution.pdf}
\caption{Action distribution for the internal (LLM) strategy in death ($n{=}74$) vs.\ non-death ($n{=}59$) episodes. Left: full distribution (both dominated by \texttt{connect}). Right: non-\texttt{connect} actions zoomed. \texttt{focus on} is $3{\times}$ more frequent in death episodes, while non-death episodes show higher rates of observation and object interaction.}
\label{fig:death-action-dist}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/death_action_timeline.pdf}
\caption{Step-by-step action sequences for 6 representative death episodes across different task types. The fatal \texttt{focus on} action (marked with arrow) consistently appears in the final steps after long \texttt{connect} sequences, illustrating the ``connect loop $\to$ fatal focus'' pattern.}
\label{fig:death-timeline}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/death_action_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Non-\texttt{connect} action distribution comparison between internal (left) and deterministic (right) strategies. The deterministic strategy shows minimal \texttt{focus on} usage across both death and non-death episodes, confirming that the action-space grounding failure is specific to LLM-generated action selection.}
\label{fig:death-comparison}
\end{figure}

\section{Ablation Studies}

